## Findings

### [P2] `instrument.py:105`+`runtime.py:208` ÔÇö #161 measures setup only on the streaming (production-default) path; the metric is silently corrupt
`with TurnContext():` wraps setup and then `return streamed` **inside** the block, so `__exit__` fires ÔÇö logging `turn completed` and resetting the `_turn` contextvar ÔÇö *before* the stream (the actual model generation) is consumed. `Runner.run_streamed` does no model work until `stream_events()` is iterated in the channel, which happens after `handle_message` returns.

I confirmed this empirically:
```
INFO:agentg.instrument:turn completed in 0.003s, 0 model calls, 2 SQL statements
--- handle_message returned; now consuming stream (the real model work) ---
DURING STREAM: _turn.get() -> None
```
So on **every production turn** (`stream_replies=True` is the default) the #161 baseline logs setup-only duration, `0 model calls`, and only the setup SQL ÔÇö and because the contextvar is already reset, model calls/SQL during generation are attributed to nothing. #161 was required to "still hold"; it does not on the production path. It's also untested there ÔÇö every `test_instrument.py` case uses `stream_replies=False`, so the gap is invisible to CI. This is a regression: pre-branch the turn was blocking, so `TurnContext` wrapped the whole `Runner.run`.

Concrete fix: keep the instrument alive across the stream on the streaming path ÔÇö e.g. have `_stream_text`/`_hold_lock` run inside the same `TurnContext` (enter in `handle_message`, exit in the outermost stream wrapper's `finally`), reusing the `TurnInstrument`/token so `_counting_acompletion` and the SQL listener attribute generation to the turn and the `turn completed` line is emitted when the stream ends. At minimum, do not emit `turn completed` on the streaming path (defer it), rather than logging a value known to be wrong. Add a streaming-path instrument test (assert `>0 model calls` after consuming the stream) so the fix is revert-proof.

### [P3] `runtime.py:31-38` ÔÇö `_drain_coach_pings` is defined in the middle of the import block
The function sits between `from agentg.demo_media import ...` and `from agentg.messages import ...`. Harmless, but it splits the imports and hurts readability. Move it below the imports with the other module-level helpers.

### [P3] `runtime.py:_hold_lock` docstring
See note above ÔÇö the "aclose before first `__anext__`" guarantee described isn't how CPython treats a never-started async generator. Not reachable via telegram, but the comment is misleading; either drop the claim or start the generator eagerly if that guarantee is actually wanted.

---

Test quality: the changed streaming/compaction/safety tests are genuinely revert-proof (they assert the new contract: `deliver_media=False` on error, lock released after `aclose`, `_compaction_done` bounded wait). The one gap is #161's streaming path, called out above.

The P2 (#161 corrupted on the production path) means the "all must still hold" bar is not met.

